ientist makes an  update the code base they will push that  to a version tracking tool such as  GitHub at that point the application  will be built from the source code  and then we're going to run automated  tests on that and this this happens  automatically once I push all that  happens and then I'll get a report back  if the tests fail I know I have to go  back and fix something if they test pass  I can then progress  um I can merge my changes into the  master brand and then we will have a  application that is ready for deployment  so let's look a little bit closer into  this first part of the CI pipeline  so it this is this in the CI build step  basically we have a data scientist is  working in our studio writing our code  and we're gonna get push and that's  going to trigger GitHub actions to kick  off the continuous integration pipeline  there's many options for CI tools we use  GitHub actions at dvo1 and then the  GitHub actions is going to build a  Docker image based on the updated code  and what's important here is that  I'm pushing my code frequently that  means that I can get instant feedback on  what each piece of code that I edit and  so I know where changes happen if I push  500 lines of code at once and something  fails it's going to take me a long time  to figure out what goes wrong but if I  push a couple lines at a time I can  isolate the problems very easily  so now let's look at this next step  we're running the automated tests  So within continuous integration tests  we have three broad categories of tests  um sort of there's this pyramid  structure so the base our unit tests  these are the most numerous pests that  we have above those we have integration  tests which are still a good amount of  those but less than unit tests and  finally there are end-to-end tests and  the test that package is very useful for  writing these type of tests  so starting at unit tests this is the  base of our testing pyramid and the goal  here is to test functions 