e but they save a  lot of time and product so if you start  a new project when you're in the  exploratory phase doing Eda initial  experiments if you start writing a lot  of tests you're going to slow yourself  down but as you progress as you get your  feature set more concrete as you get  closer to production you should start  introducing first unit tests later  integration tests and once you're really  close to production to releasing a  microservice you're going to want to  make sure that the end-to-end tests are  properly configured in  so next we're going to talk about  continuous deployment so at the end of  The Continuous integration pipeline we  have our container that's ready for  deployment  and what is going to happen here is that  this pipeline is going to take that  container and it's going to push it  through our different environment here  at dvo1 we have a staging release and  productive environment there's many ways  to do continuous deployment this is just  one example so once the container is  ready it's going to be pushed to staging  and in staging there will be some  end-to-end tests that run and we'll just  make sure that the whole application  looks good as a whole that if that goes  well it moves on to release and then  finally it'll go to production and and  this is important because  we have a customer facing application  and we have zero tolerance for any  failures or for downtime so we really  need to make sure that we have a lot of  steps in place to catch any errors uh  that might come up  and each of these environments the  staging release in production is a full  replica of the product environment you  it has our web application it has the r  microservice as well as all the other  services that make up the application  so let's talk a little bit the last  concept which is  um cluster Computing so in the cloud a  common way to run jobs is in kubernetes  so here we have our production cluster  in which we have three replicas of our  mac